# overture-protocol — Intent Tree (this.i)
#
# Source of truth for why this protocol is shaped the way it is. Record each consequential
# decision here first, in its own commit, before the text or code it justifies.

Let an untrusted party propose actions to a principal's agent = goal:
  nid: 8xxflcz6
  why: >
    Overture lets an external party, authenticated by a key but not necessarily identified,
    propose an action to an agent acting for a named principal, and receive an immediate receipt
    or refusal, pollable status, and a final disposition with verifiable evidence. It is a
    standalone, transport-independent message family. We rejected extending IPEX, because IPEX
    assumes the parties are already connected and covers only credential disclosure. We also
    rejected adopting DIDComm coprotocols (Aries RFC 0478/0482), because they assume caller and
    callee share one trust domain and have no decision step, status or cancel. Tradeoff accepted:
    a new set of messages that the KERI ecosystem must review and adopt, rather than a small
    extension to one it already knows.
  children:
    Overture messages are KERI v2 exn routes = decision:
      nid: 7cw0yhrk
      why: >-
        Every Overture message is a KERI v2 exn whose route sits under /overture/. The KERI spec defines exn as a generic exchange whose route names the transaction type and step, and IPEX already lives this way, so a route is the intended extension point. In keripy a new route is one handler registered with Exchanger.addHandler, and it inherits signing, CESR attachments, sender authentication, evidence hooks, transport neutrality and KRAM replay protection. We rejected a dedicated message family (a new ilk), because it needs a KERI spec change and parser changes in every implementation, which puts adoption on someone else's timeline; its only gain, top-level fields shaped to Overture, the a block already gives. We chose v2 over v1 for the ri recipient field and KRAM's v2 handling. Tradeoff accepted: a bet on v2 exn adoption while parts of the ecosystem still speak v1.
    Ship first as a stand-alone package that plugs into keripy = decision:
      nid: 630f824j
      why: >-
        The reference code is a separate package that registers Overture route handlers into any keripy Exchanger, and the stand-in agent runs on it. Both ends need the code: requesters are often Signify edge clients (signify-ts) or keripy users, and the agent role matches what KERIA hosts. We rejected building it inside keripy or KERIA from the start, because that makes the design wait on upstream review before it has been exercised, and a plug-in needs nobody's fork or approval. Upstreaming to keripy (building and checking messages), KERIA (hosting) and signify-ts (requesters) follows once the design has held up. Tradeoff accepted: for a while, Overture is a dependency adopters add themselves rather than something already in their stack.
    Requesters run KERI and CESR; there is no thin-requester profile = decision:
      nid: 0p27s4g9
      why: >-
        A requester must sign a proposal as a v2 exn (KERI version string, Blake3-256 SAID, CESR signatures, v2 nesting), verify the agent's receipts and status reports against the agent's transferable AID, which means resolving its KEL, and often check KERI/ACDC outcome artifacts (R18). So Overture assumes a KERI/CESR stack on both sides. An Ed25519 key of the kind fiki uses can still be the requester's first identity (R23), and RFC 9421 via fiki remains the HTTP binding's transport authentication (R22). We rejected a thin-requester profile that signs with a CESR subset and trusts fiki response signatures for live checks, because the logic a requester must support is too much to carry outside a KERI stack, and verifying the agent without its KEL had no clean answer. We rejected a JSON-only proposal signed with fiki primitives, because it contradicts the exn envelope (7cw0yhrk, 40r080ck) and loses transport-independent non-repudiation. Tradeoff accepted: a party with only fiki cannot be an Overture requester.
    A hop passes an overture on by attaching it, never by forwarding or wrapping = decision:
      nid: 4uvlnnx4
      why: >-
        When a party passes an overture onward (escalation through any number of agent layers, or the double-consent introduction), it sends its own overture, signed by itself with its own nonce and date, and attaches the original signed overture unaltered as a v2 nested substream, committing to it by digest so its own signature covers it. Each hop is the requester of the next, and only each hop's own send is judged for freshness, so a slow layer never makes the overture stale, and the original requester's date survives inside the original. keripy already does exactly this kind of nesting: its v2 /fwd envelope carries a whole signed exn as a nest, keeps the child's signatures, and commits to it in a.evt (tests/app/test_forwarding.py). We rejected forwarding the original as a live send, because the next agent would judge it for freshness against the original date and refuse it after any delay, and could not tell who passed it on. We rejected wrapping it in a message that says nothing on its own behalf, because that needs a second date inside the overture and makes the relay an anonymous carrier. How agents behind the counter talk is still out of scope; this applies when they choose to speak Overture. Tradeoff accepted: every hop keeps track of which overture below it each of its own overtures was triggered by.
    The engine authenticates only the signature on the Overture message = decision:
      nid: 48r9sm0z
      why: >-
        A party is identified by its identifier (an AID), and authenticated by its signature on an Overture message (an overture, poll or abort), verified against that identifier's key state; who controls the identifier may stay unknown. Keys are not identity: a transferable AID outlives its keys. The Overture engine bases every decision (admission, status visibility, honoring an abort, what reaches the principal) on that one signature. A transport signature, such as RFC 9421 over HTTP, authenticates only the transport exchange to the transport layer, which may use it for transport decisions alone: rate limits, blocking abusive callers, and cheap refusal before parsing CESR. Overture neither requires nor forbids the transport signer to be the requester; an agent may require a match as its own policy. We rejected requiring a match, because it would break hops that pass overtures on (4uvlnnx4), relays, and transports with no signature layer (BLE, NFC). We rejected letting the transport signer inform engine decisions, because that would make the transport layer a party to the protocol. Tradeoff accepted: an HTTP-layer allow-list cannot by itself stand in for Overture admission.
    Requesters find and trust an agent through KERI endpoint authorization = decision:
      nid: 0svwkvee
      why: >-
        A requester finds an agent, and learns that it is the principal's, through KERI's existing endpoint authorization reached by OOBI: the principal signs a /end/role/add reply naming the agent's AID in the agent role (withdrawn by /end/role/cut), and the agent signs a /loc/scheme reply giving its URL. This proves only that the agent is an endpoint the principal named, never what the agent may decide or sign; that authority is shown separately, by delegation or GCD chains. A principal may name more than one agent: keripy keys endpoint authorizations by controller, role and endpoint AID, and its endsFor returns every endpoint AID per role, with agent among the defined roles. We rejected an Overture-specific discovery or registration message, because KERI already provides a signed, revocable, BADA-governed one that every KERI stack resolves. Tradeoff accepted: discovery inherits OOBI's bootstrap weaknesses, which KERI's BADA checks already address.
    A requester keeps an interaction at the endpoint where it began = decision:
      nid: 43lr8z7v
      why: >-
        A principal may authorize several agents (0svwkvee), and an overture's identity is scoped only to the requester (7erf49uk). So the protocol requires a requester, once it has sent an overture to an endpoint of the principal, to send every later message about that overture (a resend, poll or abort) to that same endpoint, or to another location published by /loc/scheme for the agent AID that receipted the overture, never to a different agent's endpoint. A requester may therefore change transport (BLE at a counter, then HTTP from home) without changing who holds the overture's state. The protocol does not require the same agent AID to answer throughout the interaction; it requires only that whichever agent AID answers is one the principal has authorized at that endpoint. How the principal's agents divide or share overture state is behind the counter, and the protocol is silent on it; this constraint is what a highly available implementation, for instance several agents load-balanced behind one endpoint with shared state, must meet. We rejected requiring shared state across all of a principal's endpoints, because it dictates the principal's internal architecture, and rejected scoping an overture's identity to requester plus agent AID, because it would tie an interaction to one agent and forbid that kind of failover. Tradeoff accepted: a requester whose endpoint becomes unreachable cannot move an interaction to another endpoint; it begins a new one.
    Endpoint means the location a requester sends to = decision:
      nid: 2mgg0fn7
      why: >-
        In Overture, an endpoint is a location, the URL a principal's agent publishes through a /loc/scheme reply, to which a requester sends messages. Any agent AID the principal has authorized at that location through /end/role/add may answer there, so one endpoint may be served by several agent AIDs. The endpoint is the agent's general KERI ingress, where key events, replies and exn messages of every route arrive (keripy mounts it at /), not an Overture-specific HTTP path; the exn route inside the message is what selects Overture (7cw0yhrk). This is what 43lr8z7v binds an interaction to. We rejected KERI's sense, where an endpoint is identified by a single eid, because under it 'same endpoint' would mean 'same agent AID' and forbid failover between agents behind one location. Tradeoff accepted: Overture's endpoint differs from KERI's eid-keyed endpoint, so the spec must define the term where it first uses it.
    Outcome is what became of an overture = decision:
      nid: 0d2s9emg
      why: >-
        Overture's term is outcome: what became of an overture, whether the principal acted on it, declined it, or it ended by an event such as expiry or an honored abort. The agent's last message about an overture reports its outcome together with any evidence; that message is named with the message set, not here. We rejected disposition, because it implies every end is a decision, and some ends are only events. We rejected using outcome as the name of the message itself, because the requirements already use outcome for the fact the message reports (the first signed report fixes the outcome), and one word must not name both. Tradeoff accepted: until the message set names that message, text that refers to it must say what it is rather than use a short name.
    'The unit is an overture: one plea for one action = decision':
      nid: 4g7vjv8w
      why: >-
        Overture's unit is an overture: a signed request asking a principal to take exactly one action (R4, R9). Identity, admission, receipts, polls, aborts and outcomes all attach to a single overture, through its SAID (7erf49uk). A batch, if v1 has one, only carries several overtures in one message and has no id or lifecycle of its own. We rejected naming the unit proposal, because the protocol's name should say what its unit is. We rejected request, because every Overture message travels inside an HTTP request, so the request was refused would be read two ways. We rejected letting overture name the batch, because then one overture would name many actions and each id would point inside it. Tradeoff accepted: earlier decisions and the requirements must be reworded from request and proposal to overture.
    A batch carries one or more overtures, each with its own lifecycle = decision:
      nid: 8zysn4nv
      why: >-
        The thing that carries one or more overtures in one message is a batch. Overtures in a batch may depend on one another: an overture references its prerequisites by SAID, the links form a dependency graph, and a prerequisite may be in the same batch (R9). Each overture is still admitted, receipted and given its outcome as itself; the batch has no identifier or lifecycle of its own (4g7vjv8w). A batch is never all-or-nothing: when a prerequisite is declined or fails, each overture that depends on it gets its own outcome, and overtures that do not depend on it proceed. A requester that wants overtures bound together expresses that with dependency links; the protocol does not impose it. We rejected all-or-nothing batches, because they would need a batch-level outcome the batch does not have, and because the dependency graph already says what is bound to what. Dependency links express "proceed only if the prerequisite succeeded", not rollback: an overture already acted on is not undone when a later one fails. We rejected package, because it already names our code's home (630f824j) and is common in KERI and keripy text; envelope, because it implies wrapping one thing, the picture 4uvlnnx4 rejected; pouch, because it says nothing to a reader before it is defined; and overture set, because it is two words and makes polls over several overtures read awkwardly. Batch has no technical meaning in KERI, keripy or HTTP, and R9 and R16 already use it. Tradeoff accepted: readers may assume a batch is atomic, so the spec must say where it defines the term what a batch does and does not bind together.
    An overture's body commits by SAID to the attachments it relies on = decision:
      nid: 8erq0hp5
      why: >-
        A requester's signature on an exn covers only the body, never the CESR attachment groups that follow it, so an attachment is under that signature only if the body names it by SAID. The body MUST so commit to each attached overture (4uvlnnx4) and each item of authority evidence (GCD chains, delegation KELs) the overture relies on, because swapping either changes what the overture asserts or exposes a credential the requester did not choose to present. The body of a status report MUST likewise commit to each result it carries as an attachment, because the report states that each result is a result of the overture, and an uncommitted result could be swapped by a relay without the report failing to verify. KELs attached for first contact need no commitment, because a KEL verifies itself; leaving them uncommitted also lets a relay or agent supply fresher key state while an overture waits. Dependency links are body fields, not attachments. An attachment the body does not name carries no claim by the requester. We rejected committing to every attachment, because it freezes key state at signing and adds bytes for no security gain, and rejected committing to none, because it lets anyone on the path change what the overture claims. Tradeoff accepted: two kinds of attachment follow two rules, and each new kind must be placed in one.

# Terms. One term per concept; `i glossary` renders these. A term is defined here, not in the requirements.

    A malformed batch is refused whole = decision:
      nid: 7f9ydc0f
      why: >-
        An agent checks a batch before admitting any of it. If the batch is malformed (a prerequisite the agent does not know, the same overture twice, or an overture whose signature or SAID commitments do not verify), the agent refuses the whole batch and admits none of it. A well-formed batch is still judged overture by overture: a policy refusal or decline of one ends only the overtures that depend on it (8zysn4nv). Malformation means the requester built the batch wrongly, so nothing in it can be trusted to mean what was intended. Whole refusal also keeps refusal cheap and stateless (R12), and lets the requester fix and resend, since nothing was admitted. We rejected admitting the well-formed part, because it could launch actions the requester believed were linked to ones that were dropped. Tradeoff accepted: one bad overture holds up unrelated good ones in the same batch, a cost borne by the requester that built it.
    A message that refers to overtures may refer to several = decision:
      nid: 0u4svfnq
      why: >-
        Every message that refers to overtures by SAID, whether a poll, an abort, or the agent's status report, MAY refer to several overtures at once. Each referenced overture is still answered or acted on separately, and nothing is all-or-nothing: an abort referencing three overtures may find one already done and withdraw the other two, and the reply says so for each. Such a message is not a batch, because a batch carries overtures, and these refer to overtures that already exist; an abort is not an overture, since it asks nothing of the principal. We rejected one message per overture, because a requester with many pending overtures would pay a round trip for each. Tradeoff accepted: each such message needs a per-overture structure in its reply.
    The agent may suggest when to poll next = decision:
      nid: 0jjt97xh
      why: >-
        A receipt and every later status report MAY carry a suggested time for the requester's next poll. It is never required, and the requester need not honor it; its purpose is to spare both sides a poll every few seconds while nothing can happen, as HTTP's Retry-After does. Because the agent computes it from what is behind the counter, it can reveal who decides or when a person is available, so it SHOULD NOT reveal behind-the-counter facts, and agents are advised to give coarse or jittered values. We rejected MUST NOT, because a principal may not care who learns how it decides, and that choice is the principal's. We rejected leaving polling cadence wholly to the requester, because only the agent knows when an answer is plausible. Tradeoff accepted: an agent that follows a principal's wish to be open about its process may disclose it through this field.
    A requester sees one state, pending, and six outcomes = decision:
      nid: 36j5xvln
      why: >-
        From admission until its outcome, an overture's only visible state is pending; an agent may track internally that the principal has agreed and the action is under way, but never shows it, because that would disclose a decision and its timing before anything is finished, and the requester could do nothing different with it. The outcomes are completed, declined, expired (its start-by passed before it was acted on), failed (agreed to but did not succeed), aborted (the requester's abort was honored), and precluded (a prerequisite did not succeed, so it was never acted on). Expired and precluded are separate from declined because they are events, not decisions (0d2s9emg). We rejected prevented for the last, because it suggests someone acted to stop the overture, and blocked, because it suggests the overture could later proceed. Tradeoff accepted: failed reveals after the fact that the principal agreed, kept because it tells the requester a retry may succeed where a decline would not.
    Every Overture message states what its signature means = decision:
      nid: 7m92u5mp
      why: >-
        No Overture signature leaves its meaning to context. Each message states its signer's stance through its signed route, its signed field names, or, where one form serves more than one stance, a signed meaning field from a closed set. Receipts are such a form: admitted (an agent has admitted an overture as a work item and acknowledges notice of its content, R13) or seen (the signer received the item, with no commitment). The stance result says an artifact is a result of an overture: the agent's status report states it for every result it names, by listing them under a signed results key; a result that can sign, such as a newly created AID, may state it about itself, and the action's catalog entry says when that second signature is required. This reverses 08ehqflx, which let a receipt's meaning come from who signed what; as Who Is Signing? argues (https://dhh1128.github.io/papers/who-sign.html), a signature proves processing, not intent, so an outsider must be able to read the intent from what was signed. We rejected bound, because it named a mechanism rather than the claim and applied to only one action. Tradeoff accepted: a meaning field and a closed set of stances to maintain.
    An overture is verified when received, without anchoring = decision:
      nid: 2j74vu2p
      why: >-
        An agent verifies an overture against the requester's key state when it receives it, and the requester need not anchor the overture's signature in its KEL; a later rotation does not undo admission. Accountability for overtures comes from the signed records the parties keep (receipts and status reports, each stating what its signature means, 7m92u5mp), not from KEL anchoring. We rejected requiring anchoring, because a non-transferable AID has no KEL to anchor in, and requesters may use one (R23), and because it would cost every requester an interaction event per overture. Tradeoff accepted: after a rotation forced by compromise, an agent cannot tell whether an overture signed before the rotation came from the requester or from the key's thief; the requester's remedy is to abort its pending overtures under its new key state, which does nothing for an overture already completed.
    Preconditions are advisory, and an unreadable one bars admission = decision:
      nid: 83e2qr0z
      why: >-
        A requester may state in advance, as preconditions, that its overture should not be started unless it can be carried out in a way that meets them. The set is open: version 1 defines start-by and finish-by, and others (a cost ceiling, a bar on public disclosure) are expected. Preconditions are advice, not obligations on the principal, and a precondition that was predicted to be meetable, but that ends unmet, can only be judged a failure by the requester; nothing in the protocol depends on proving when or how well something finished. An agent must not admit an overture carrying a precondition it does not understand, because advice can be set aside deliberately only by an agent that can read it, and this rule cannot be added later to agents already deployed. We rejected letting agents ignore unknown preconditions, because a later precondition such as a bar on public disclosure would then be silently violated by every earlier agent. Tradeoff accepted: a requester that uses a new precondition reaches only agents that understand it, and can send again without it.
    The signer requirement is a precondition, defaulting to representative = decision:
      nid: 5yen7mup
      why: >-
        A requester's statement of which signer it will accept on a result is said in advance, so it is a precondition (83e2qr0z) and advisory like the rest: an agent should decline rather than start when it can see a result would not be signed as required, and the requester checks the signer of any result itself. Its values are principal (the principal's AID, or a delegate created with the Delegate-Is-Delegator trait) and representative (the principal, or a signer proving a chain of representation to it by KERI delegation or GCD credentials linked through issuer edges). An overture without a signer precondition is treated as carrying representative, and the spec states this so the overture's signature carries no meaning left to context (7m92u5mp). We rejected having no default, because a principal could properly have a third party with no chain to it (a bank or notary) sign a result, which is not the principal's act and not what a requester addressing that principal (R3) would expect. We rejected the earlier MUST NOT complete, because a wrongly signed result cannot bind the requester (R4) and the requester checks it anyway. Tradeoff accepted: a requester that wants a third party's signature must say so with a richer precondition, which version 1 does not define.
    Audience is a precondition = decision:
      nid: 35d29vn5
      why: >-
        A requester asking for a result bound to one downstream relying party (referenced by AID) and that party's nonce is saying in advance not to start unless the result can be bound that way, so audience is a precondition (83e2qr0z), advisory like the rest; the requester checks the binding itself. The result does the binding and the overture only carries the inputs. The nonce is the relying party's challenge: Overture carries it unchanged as an opaque value and does not name it u, because u is entropy an author makes up to make its own block's SAID unique, while this value is chosen by another party so that it comes back in the result; relying parties are advised to make it unpredictable. Audience is not the party on whose behalf the requester acts, which the requester's attached authority evidence shows (8erq0hp5). We rejected making audience a field of each catalog entry, because binding to an audience cuts across every action whose result is a signed artifact, as the signer requirement does (5yen7mup). Tradeoff accepted: an action whose result cannot bind an audience can only be declined when audience is set.
    Purpose is the requester's signed claim, not a gate = decision:
      nid: 6yusrcwc
      why: >-
        An overture may carry a purpose, text the requester writes for a person saying why it asks. It is the requester's claim, covered by its signature, and the requester answers for it; no agent checks it as a condition of admission. It is optional, because a requester cannot know whether its overture will reach a person, and an agent may decline one without it as its own policy. A standard description of what the action does comes from the action's catalog entry, not from the requester. How an agent shows an overture to its principal is behind the counter, so the rule that a person approving should see the exact signed overture, never only a summary rendered from it, is advice to implementers rather than a protocol requirement. We rejected making that a protocol requirement, because the protocol does not govern what happens behind the counter (66u9sk4r). Tradeoff accepted: the main defence against a requester using the purpose to mislead a person rests on implementations following advice.
    Catalog entries sit on the GCD act grid = decision:
      nid: 0nztkj2q
      why: >-
        Each action catalog entry has a stable identifier, a parameter schema, a description a person can read (6yusrcwc), and the (effect, state-kind) points the action occupies on the act grid that GCD credentials constrain through acts (https://github.com/bakobo/schema/blob/main/gcd/index.md); an action may occupy several points. Each entry also says which of its parameters designate the act's targets, the grid's third coordinate, because GCD treats acts and targets as one constraint and a policy engine cannot check an act without knowing what it lands on. Placing actions on that grid lets a policy engine behind the counter check an overture against a GCD without knowing Overture. New entries need no protocol change, and an agent refuses before admission an overture for an action it does not know, as it does an unknown precondition (83e2qr0z). We rejected an Overture-specific classification of actions, because GCD already defines one that delegates' authority is written in. Tradeoff accepted: the catalog depends on the GCD grid's vocabulary, and changes there reach the catalog.
    A result's links are shown by the outcome report and representation evidence = decision:
      nid: 4662pten
      why: >-
        Every result is a verifiable artifact or a reference that resolves to one, and verifying it needs nothing specific to Overture. That a result is a result of the overture is stated by the status report that gives the outcome (7m92u5mp), which commits to each result it carries (8erq0hp5); a result that can sign may state the same about itself, and a catalog entry may require that. That a result such as a new AID represents the principal is shown by the same representation evidence the signer precondition accepts (5yen7mup): KERI delegation or a GCD chain. We rejected a separate requirement that "where the link is the result, the link is signed", and the bound receipt it used, because those two mechanisms already sign both links. Which proof the relationship-AID action requires by default is a question for that action's catalog entry. Tradeoff accepted: a requester that discards the status report keeps the results but loses the signed claim tying them to the overture.
    An overture's exn carries the principal's AID in ri = decision:
      nid: 6f7mlrg6
      why: >-
        The v2 exn that carries overtures puts the principal's AID in ri, so the envelope itself references the principal (R3) and says what R1 says: the overture is addressed to the principal and delivered to an endpoint the principal authorized (0svwkvee). An agent compares ri with its principal's AID and refuses before admission an overture addressed to any other. keripy's Exchanger dispatches on the route and never reads ri; only route handlers do, and Overture's are its own (630f824j). KERIA addresses the AIDs it hosts the same way. We rejected the agent's AID in ri with a principal field in the a block, because it makes two references do one job and ties the overture to one agent, which R3 deliberately does not. Tradeoff accepted: this differs from IPEX, whose handlers expect ri to be the local hab, and a future keripy rule dropping any exn whose ri is not a local hab would break a stand-alone agent (not a KERIA-style host).
    The overture message carries a batch of self-addressed overtures = decision:
      nid: 5xwgkz20
      why: >-
        The exn that carries overtures leaves x, p and q empty, because an overture's own SAID already ties later messages to it (7erf49uk), and its a block holds overtures, a list of one or more (the batch, 8zysn4nv), and optionally waitUntil, the time until which the agent is asked to hold the exchange open (R16). Each overture is an object with its own SAID in d, which stays the same across resends while the send's SAID changes with dt and identifies the overture (7erf49uk). Each carries u, action and params, and optionally prereqs, preconditions (a map, so an unknown precondition is detectable, R5: startBy, finishBy, signer, audience with aid and nonce), purpose, triggeredBy and evidence. triggeredBy holds the SAID of the overture that caused this one, which must be attached whole as a nested substream (4uvlnnx4); it expresses the trigger relation for escalation and introduction alike. evidence lists the SAIDs of the authority evidence relied on (8erq0hp5). Overture-specific fields have expressive camelCase names; envelope fields keep keripy's. We rejected passesOn (it can mean "declines"), relays (in DIDComm and here a relay only carries), parent (on an escalation path it reads as a supervising agent) and prior (it collides with exn p and suggests age). Tradeoff accepted: a hop that relays an overture from a batch must attach the whole batch (F-NAK4).
    A receipt message lists receipts, and nextPoll covers only what it references = decision:
      nid: 9z0lpky7
      why: >-
        A receipt message's a block holds receipts, a list of items, each with its own meaning (admitted or seen, R17), since one message may receipt several things. An admitted receipt carries said, the overture's own SAID (7erf49uk), which is also the digest of the admitted content; a seen receipt carries said, the exn SAID of the message seen. The field is said rather than d because d in an object conventionally holds that object's own SAID. The envelope's i is the signer, ri the party receipted to, and dt the signer's time, which on an admitted receipt is the time of admission. A suggested next-poll time (0jjt97xh) is nextPoll, one per message, and its scope is explicit: the still-pending overtures the message references by SAID, and no others, the same scope as R16's waitUntil hint. Only the agent computes it, so it appears only in messages the agent signs (admitted receipts, status reports, answers to aborts) and is omitted when nothing referenced is pending. We rejected a global scope (no poll before T about anything), because the agent must track every pending overture of the requester and the requester learns nothing about which to ask about, and an implicit scope, because the protocol has no grouping beyond what a message references. We rejected a hint per overture, because separate times show which overture waits on something slow, typically a person. Tradeoff accepted: when one poll mixes fast and slow overtures, the hint follows the fast one and the slow ones are polled early.
    Polls and aborts list overture SAIDs, and waiting is until an absolute time = decision:
      nid: 5zj9qmg0
      why: >-
        A poll and an abort are each signed by the requester, with i the requester's AID and ri the principal's AID (as 6f7mlrg6), and are sent to the agent that receipted the overtures (R2). Each a block holds saids, the SAIDs of the overtures concerned, read in the requester's own scope (R14, R16); a poll may add waitUntil. Both are answered by a status report. They stay two messages, not one with a flag, because the route states what the signature means (7m92u5mp), and asking about overtures and withdrawing them are different stances. The hint for holding an exchange open is waitUntil, an absolute time, in the overture message and the poll: the agent holds the exchange open until that time by its own clock, and answers at once if it has passed. We rejected a relative wait in seconds, because its reference point is the moment of sending, which the agent can only guess, and on store-and-forward links the guess can be off by hours; and we rejected deriving the deadline from dt plus seconds, because it leaves the meaning implicit. Absolute times also match startBy, finishBy and nextPoll. Clock agreement is already assumed, since KRAM refuses a send whose dt lies outside the receiver's window. An abort carries no waitUntil, because its answer reports the state at the moment it arrives. Tradeoff accepted: a sender with a skewed clock gets a shorter or longer hold than it meant, within KRAM's window.
    The agent reports states and outcomes in one message, the status report = decision:
      nid: 30urjn80
      why: >-
        The agent's reports about overtures are one message, the status report. It has one item per overture it references, each with a single status: pending, unknown, or one of the six outcomes (36j5xvln). It answers a poll, an abort, and an overture sent with waitUntil, and it is what the agent sends when it delivers on its own initiative (R16). An item that gives an outcome carries the statement that each result it names is a result of the overture (7m92u5mp) and commits to the results it carries (8erq0hp5); an item that says pending carries none. This names the message 0d2s9emg left unnamed. We rejected a separate outcome report, because an outcome is only the last status, and a poll about several overtures can find some pending and some ended, which would split one answer across two messages or need two kinds of reply. Tradeoff accepted: every earlier use of outcome report becomes status report, and one message type carries two signature stances, distinguished per item by its status.
    An overture is identified by its own SAID = decision:
      nid: 7erf49uk
      why: >-
        An overture is identified by its own SAID, the d of the overture object. The SAID covers everything in the overture but not the send's dt, so every resend of the same content carries the same SAID, and different content is by construction a different overture. Each overture carries u, a salty nonce of about 128 bits of entropy that the requester makes fresh for each overture: the KERI spec's u, which makes the SAID of the enclosing message universally unique (as in xip), and what ACDC v1.1 calls unique entropy. Because the overture's SAID travels in receipts, status reports, prereqs and triggeredBy, the entropy also keeps that SAID from revealing a guessable overture's content. A counter would make overtures unique but not blind them, so u is never a counter. With u, so two identical asks, such as two hamburgers, are two overtures, and an identical ask made a year later is not matched to last year's. Identity is scoped to the requester's AID: an overture with the same SAID sent by another requester is that requester's own. Prerequisite links, polls, aborts, receipts and status reports reference an overture by its SAID. A prerequisite's SAID must exist before an overture that depends on it can be hashed, so a dependency cycle cannot be built. This replaces 69tz0ngx, which identified an overture by a requester-chosen overture id, and 8nyrml77, which made the first version admitted under an id authoritative; together they allowed different content under one id and needed a rule for it, and no use case needed that. We rejected an optional nonce, because a requester that omitted it would have a later identical ask matched to an earlier, possibly completed, overture. Tradeoff accepted: references are 44-character SAIDs where an id might have been a dozen characters, and a requester that changes its mind sends a new overture and aborts the old one.
    The status report lists one status per overture = decision:
      nid: 8ryggr28
      why: >-
        The status report (30urjn80) is signed by the agent, with i the agent's AID and ri the requester's. Its a block holds statuses, one item per overture it references, each with said (the overture's SAID, 7erf49uk) and status (pending, unknown, or one of the six outcomes, 36j5xvln). An item that gives an outcome may carry results, each either {said} for an artifact, the body thereby committing to it when attached (8erq0hp5), optionally with registry for a credential checked by reference, or {aid, oobi} for a new identifier (R18); an aborted item's results list anything already done. reason is optional free text, given only when the principal chooses (R15). nextPoll follows 9z0lpky7 and covers only the items still pending. We rejected also carrying a requester-scoped id beside said, because SAID identity leaves one reference doing one job. Tradeoff accepted: every item carries a 44-character SAID.
facet = term:
  id: 7v6n52dz
  definition: >-
    One of the many aspects in which a single entity acts, kept isolated from its other facets, so that only the entity decides who may learn that two facets are the same entity. Each facet has its own AID, and a principal is always one facet. See Identity Facets, https://dhh1128.github.io/papers/if.html.
overture = term:
  id: 2q2c2dfl
  definition: >-
    A signed request from a requester asking a principal to take exactly one action. Its own SAID, which covers a nonce the requester makes fresh for it, identifies it within the requester's scope, so two sends carrying the same SAID are the same overture (7erf49uk, 4g7vjv8w).
batch = term:
  id: 54m97zvg
  definition: >-
    A single message that carries one or more overtures. Overtures in a batch may depend on one another through dependency links, but each keeps its own lifecycle, and a batch is never all-or-nothing (8zysn4nv).
outcome = term:
  id: 8hf8lm3p
  definition: >-
    What became of an overture: the principal acted on it or declined it, or it ended by an event such as expiry or an honored abort. The agent reports the outcome, with any evidence, in its last message about that overture (0d2s9emg).
endpoint = term:
  id: 75jkh39e
  definition: >-
    The location a requester sends to: the URL a principal's agent publishes by a /loc/scheme reply, which is the agent's general KERI ingress. Any agent AID the principal has authorized there by /end/role/add may answer. This differs from KERI's sense, in which an endpoint is identified by one eid (2mgg0fn7).
requester = term:
  id: 5dnehv9p
  definition: >-
    The party that sends an overture, identified by its AID (R23). Who controls that AID may remain unknown.
agent = term:
  id: 4m52t924
  definition: >-
    A party, identified by its AID, that receives overtures for exactly one principal and is the only party that answers the requester in Overture. The principal authorizes it as an endpoint, and may authorize several agents (0svwkvee). An agent refuses, before admission, an overture that references a principal it does not act for (R3).
principal = term:
  id: 2k8fxl77
  definition: >-
    The AID an agent acts for: one facet of whatever entity stands behind it. An overture references it (R3), but it never takes part in the protocol.
behind the counter = term:
  id: 66u9sk4r
  definition: >-
    Everything between the agent and the principal, such as a policy engine, escalation to the principal, or nested layers of agents. The requester deals only with whoever answers at the endpoint, as a customer deals with whoever stands at a service counter, and the protocol never discloses what is behind it.
